<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/17th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · War</p><h1 class="enc-title">Anglo-French War (1627–1629)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1627">1627</a>–<a href="/years/1629">1629</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · <a href="/encyclopedia/wars">All wars</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Anglo-French War of 1627–1629 was fought between <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> under Charles I and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> under Louis XIII, partly in support of the French <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>. An English expedition under the Duke of Buckingham to the Île de Ré failed in 1627, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> surrendered to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cardinal-richelieu">Cardinal Richelieu</a>'s siege in 1628; the Treaty of Susa (1629) restored peace.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/anglo-french-war-1627-1629.jpg" alt="Anglo-French War (1627–1629)" width="320" height="182" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Henri-Paul Motte · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Siege_of_La_Rochelle_1881_Henri_Motte.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The war followed the failure of the Anglo-French alliance of 1624, through which <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> had hoped to find in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> an ally against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-habsburg">House of Habsburg</a>. Cardinal Richelieu came to power the same year, and in 1625 he used English warships to defeat the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a> at the Recovery of Ré island, which caused outrage in England. In 1626 France made a secret peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, quarrels arose over the household of Henrietta Maria, wife of Charles I, and France began to build up its navy. From March 1627 Walter Montagu worked to organise a rebellion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a>, the centre of Huguenot sea power.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The fighting was mainly at sea. In June 1627 Charles I's favourite, George Villiers, 1st Duke of Buckingham, landed 6,000 men from a fleet of 80 ships on the Île de Ré and laid siege to the fortified town of Saint-Martin-de-Ré. Small French boats kept it supplied through the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naval-blockade">blockade</a>, and after three months, short of money and weakened by disease, the English were repulsed with heavy losses. In April 1628 a fleet under William Feilding, 1st Earl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denbigh">Denbigh</a>, returned to Portsmouth without a fight, and on the way home lost three ships burnt and four captured to a French squadron off Cherbourg.</p><p>A second fleet of 29 warships and 31 merchantmen, prepared by Buckingham just before his assassination, sailed under Robert Bertie, 1st Earl of Lindsey, in August 1628, but in September it failed to force the sea wall, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/la-rochelle">La Rochelle</a> surrendered on 28 October 1628. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-america">North America</a> an English force under David Kirke occupied Tadoussac and Cap Tourmente in 1628, captured a supply convoy and a relief fleet, and compelled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/samuel-de-champlain">Samuel de Champlain</a> to surrender <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> on 19 July 1629.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>After the Peace of Alès of April 1629 settled terms with the defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/huguenots">Huguenots</a>, Richelieu sought to end the war with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> so as to break the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> encirclement of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. The peace amounted to little more than a return to the state before the war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quebec">Quebec</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acadia">Acadia</a> were handed back to the French Company of One Hundred Associates by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-saint-germain-en-laye-1632">Treaty of Saint-Germain-en-Laye</a> of 1632, in return for the payment of Henrietta Maria's dowry. England also made peace with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in 1630, and its withdrawal from European affairs dismayed Protestant forces on the continent, while by 1630 the French navy was larger than England's.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Anglo-French War (1627–1629)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>It came after the <b>Anglo-French War</b> of 1627 to 1629 had ended, and the colony was again in French hands.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/march-29">29 March in history</a></li></ul></section>
